findElement vs findElements

1) findElement:-
---------------
Finds a single WebElement.
Returns the first matching element on the page.
If element is not found → throws NoSuchElementException.

2) findElements:-
----------------
Finds multiple WebElements.
Returns a List<WebElement>.
If no element found → returns empty list (size = 0).
Does NOT throw exception.

Exceptions in Selenium:-
-----------------------

1) NoSuchElementException:- When element is not present in the DOM (page source).

Usually caused by:-
Wrong locator
Page not fully loaded
Frame not switched
Dynamic element not yet loaded

2) ElementNotInteractableException:- Element is present in DOM but cannot be interacted with.

Element may be:-
Hidden
Disabled
Covered by another element
Not yet ready for interaction

3) InvalidElementStateException:- When element state is not appropriate for action.

Trying to type into:-
Disabled input field
Read-only field
Trying to clear a non-editable field

4) StaleElementReferenceException:-

Occurs when the element you previously located is no longer attached to the DOM.
This usually happens when the page refreshes, reloads, or dynamically updates the element.

Page refresh or navigation
DOM updated via JavaScript/AJAX
Element re-rendered (React/Angular apps)
Switching between frames/windows

NoSuchFrameException - When we try to switch to a frame but the targeted frame is not present.
NoSuchWindowException - When we try to switch to a window but the targeted window is not present.
NoSuchAttributeException - When we are trying to fetch an attribute's value but the attribute is not correct

TimeoutException - When a command execution gets timeout.
WebDriverException - When there is some issue with driver instance preventing it from getting launched

Handling Dynamic Web Elements in Selenium:–

Use dynamic XPath with contains() or starts-with() when attributes change.
Avoid absolute XPath; prefer relative XPath.
Use stable attributes like name, class, placeholder, or data-*.
Use Explicit Wait (WebDriverWait) for dynamically loaded elements.
Handle StaleElementReferenceException by re-locating the element.
Use indexing carefully if multiple similar elements exist.
Use JavaScriptExecutor if normal click fails.
For dynamic tables, use custom XPath based on text.

