Day-27

Wait commands: -
--------------

Synchronization Issue:-
----------------------
When Selenium executes faster than the web application loads, it throws errors like:
NoSuchElementException, ElementNotInteractableException, TimeoutException. To avoid this, We use Waits.

1) Implicit Wait:- Implicit Wait tells Selenium to wait for a specific amount of time before throwing NoSuchElementException.
-----------------
driver.manage().timeouts().implicitlyWait(Duration.ofSeconds(10));

Uses:-
1) Applied only once.
2) It will not wait till maximum time if the element is available
3) Applicable for all the elements
4) Easy to use
5) Default value = 0 seconds.
6) if the time is not sufficient then you will get exception

2) Explicit Wait:- Explicit Wait tells Selenium to wait until a specific condition occurs before proceeding further.
-----------------
WebDriverWait wait = new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10));
WebElement loginBtn = wait.until(ExpectedConditions.visibilityOfElementLocated(By.id("login")));

Uses:-
1) Conditional based, it will work more effectively.
2) Finding element is inclusive( for some conditions)
3) It will wait for condition to be true, then consider the time
4) We need to write multiple statements for multiple elements
5) More flexible

Common Conditions:-
------------------

visibilityOfElementLocated
elementToBeClickable
presenceOfElementLocated
titleContains
alertIsPresent

3) Fluent Wait:- Fluent Wait is an advanced explicit wait with polling and exception handling.
---------------
Wait<WebDriver> wait = new FluentWait<WebDriver>(driver)
   .withTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(time))
   .pollingEvery(Duration.ofSeconds(time))
   .ignoring(NoSuchElementException.class);

WebElement foo = wait.until(new Function<WebDriver, WebElement>() {
   public WebElement apply(WebDriver driver) {
       return driver.findElement(By.id("foo"));
   }
});

Uses:-
1) Checks condition every polling interval (e.g., 2 seconds).
2) Useful for dynamic elements.
3) Most flexible wait.

